<chapter
 id="lecture08">
#title("Persistent Arrays and References")

#para("\

Let us start with references. A reference is precisely an
(initialized) singleton array, that is, an array of size 1, and its
typical use is for implementing a global variable that can be updated.
In the absence of GC, there is no function for allocating a reference
on heap: a reference can only be created from memory that is never
freed, as a toplevel variable is. The function #dyncode("ref_make_viewptr")
turns the address of such a variable (together with the proof that the
variable is at that address) into a reference.

")

#para("\

The functions #dyncode("ref_get_elt") and
#dyncode("ref_set_elt") are for fetching and updating the value stored
in a reference, respectively.

Both #dyncode("!") and #dyncode("[]") are overloaded with
#dyncode("ref_get_elt") and #dyncode("ref_set_elt"). For instance, the
following code prints out 0, 1, and 3 twice:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
var r0_var: int = 0
val r0 = ref_make_viewptr{int}(view@ r0_var | addr@ r0_var)
//
val () = println! (!r0)
val () = (!r0 := !r0 + 1)
val () = println! (!r0)
val () = (!r0 := !r0 + 2)
val () = println! (!r0)
//
var r1_var: int = 0
val r1 = ref_make_viewptr{int}(view@ r1_var | addr@ r1_var)
//
val () = println! (r1[])
val () = (r1[] := r1[] + 1)
val () = println! (r1[])
val () = (r1[] := r1[] + 2)
val () = println! (r1[])
//
')

")

#para("\

Programmers often misuse references. This is especially true for those
with a background in imperative programming. For instance, the
following example shows a typical poor use of references that is often
resulted from someone learning functional programming by \"translating\"
code written in imperative style: two references are created for each call to
a function that computes the factorial, and they are used for holding a counter and the
product. In this dialect of ATS, such a function cannot even be written, as a
reference cannot be made from the local variables of a function (which are
freed when the call returns). If the imperative style is really wanted, a local
variable of the function can be used, which lives in the stack-frame and is
passed by reference (the symbol #dyncode("&amp;")) to a function that updates it:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
fact_var
(n: int): int = let
//
var i: int = 0
var r: int = 1
//
fun
loop
(n: int, i: &int >> _, r: &int >> _): void =
  if i < n then (i := i + 1; r := r * i; loop(n, i, r))
//
val () = loop(n, i, r)
//
in
  r
end (* end of [fact_var] *)
//
val () = println! ("fact_var(10) = ", fact_var(10))
//
')

Compared to a tail-recursive implementation of the factorial
function in functional style, #dyncode("fact_var") needs variables in memory that are
updated, while the tail-recursive function passes its accumulator in a register.
The version in functional style is preferable.

")

#para("\

An array is a linear value in this dialect of ATS: the type
#stacode("arrayptr(T, n)") is for an array of #stacode("n") cells storing
elements of the type T, and the owner of the array is to free it by calling
#dyncode("arrayptr_free"). The array does not store its size
(the size is a static term that is known to the typechecker), and the
functions below are passed the size along with the array, like in C. The array0-values of
the GC era (an array together with its size, shared and reclaimed by GC) are not
available. The following function is called to create an array:

#sats2xhtml('\
//
fun
{a:t0p}
arrayptr_make_elt
  {n:int}
  (asz: size_t(n), x: a):<!wrt> arrayptr(a, n)
// end of [arrayptr_make_elt]
//
')

Given a non-negative integer #dyncode("asz") and an element
#dyncode("x"), #dyncode("arrayptr_make_elt") returns an array
of size #dyncode("asz") in which each of its cells is
initialized with #dyncode("x").

")

#para("\

The functions #dyncode("arrayptr_get_at") and #dyncode("arrayptr_set_at")
are for fetching and updating the value stored in an array at a
given index. The index is required (by the type system) to be between 0 and
the array size minus 1, inclusive, and so no exception is needed for an invalid
index: the program is rejected if the typechecker cannot make sure that the
index is valid. Also please note that the brackets #dyncode("[]") are overloaded with
both #dyncode("arrayptr_get_at") and #dyncode("arrayptr_set_at"). For
instance, evaluating the following code prints out two lines: the
first consisting of the text #dyncode("000") and the second consisting
of the text #dyncode("123"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val A =
arrayptr_make_elt<int>(i2sz(3), 0)
//
val () =
println! (A[0], A[1], A[2])
//
val () = A[0] := 1
val x0 = A[0]
val () = A[1] := x0 + 1
val x1 = A[1]
val () = A[2] := x1 + 1
//
val () =
println! (A[0], A[1], A[2])
//
')

An array that is read twice in an expression (the expression
#dyncode("A[1] := A[0] + 1") for instance) is borrowed twice in that
expression, which the typechecker refuses; the value that is read is bound first.

")

#para("\

Another commonly used function for creating an array is
#dyncode("arrayptr_tabulate"). The function that gives the content of the cell of
each index is not an argument but a template parameter,
#dyncode("array_tabulate$fopr"), which is given an implementation by the
caller for the type of the elements. The following code makes the array
consisting of the squares of the integers from 0 to 4:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// The array is created by [arrayptr_tabulate], which calls
// [array_tabulate$fopr] to make the element of each index
//
implement
array_tabulate$fopr<int>(i) = sz2i(i) * sz2i(i)
//
val B = arrayptr_tabulate<int>(i2sz(5))
//
val () = (fprint_arrayptr<int>(stdout_ref, B, i2sz(5)); print_newline())
val () = arrayptr_free(B)
//
')

")

#para("\

Like lists, there are many commonly used functions for
processing arrays. For instance, the following function
#dyncode("arrayptr_foreach") corresponds to the previouly presented
#dyncode("list_foreach"). The array is borrowed, its size is passed, and the function
argument is a flat closure that is passed by reference:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t0p}
arrayptr_foreach
  {n:nat}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), asz: int(n), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void
) : void // end of [arrayptr_foreach]
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
arrayptr_foreach{n}(A, asz, fwork) =
loop(A, 0, fwork) where
{
fun
loop
{i:nat | i <= n}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), i: int(i), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void
) : void =
  if i < asz then (fwork(A[i]); loop(A, i+1, fwork)) else ()
} (* end of [arrayptr_foreach] *)
//
')

The following function
#dyncode("arrayptr_foldleft") corresponds to the previouly presented
#dyncode("list_foldleft"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{r:t0p}
{a:t0p}
arrayptr_foldleft
  {n:nat}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), asz: int(n), r0: r, fopr: &(r, a) -<clo1> r
) : r // end of [arrayptr_foldleft]
//
implement
{r}{a}
arrayptr_foldleft
  {n}(A, asz, r0, fopr) =
loop(A, 0, r0, fopr) where
{
fun
loop
{i:nat | i <= n}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), i: int(i), r0: r, fopr: &(r, a) -<clo1> r
) : r =
  if i < asz then loop(A, i+1, fopr(r0, A[i]), fopr) else r0
} (* end of [arrayptr_foldleft] *)
//
')

The following function
#dyncode("arrayptr_foldright") corresponds to the previouly presented
#dyncode("list_foldright"):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{r:t0p}
{a:t0p}
arrayptr_foldright
  {n:nat}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), asz: int(n), fopr: &(a, r) -<clo1> r, r0: r
) : r // end of [arrayptr_foldright]
//
implement
{r}{a}
arrayptr_foldright
  {n}(A, asz, fopr, r0) =
loop(A, asz, fopr, r0) where
{
fun
loop
{i:nat | i <= n}
(
A: !arrayptr(a, n), i: int(i), fopr: &(a, r) -<clo1> r, r0: r
) : r =
  if i > 0 then loop(A, i-1, fopr, fopr(A[i-1], r0)) else r0
} (* end of [arrayptr_foldright] *)
//
')

Unlike #dyncode("list_foldright"), #dyncode("arrayptr_foldright")
is tail-recursive. A test of the three functions:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val () =
{
var fwork =
lam@(x: int): void => print!(x, ";")
val () = arrayptr_foreach<int>(A, 3, fwork)
val () = println! ()
}
//
val () =
{
var fopr = lam@(r: int, x: int): int => r * 10 + x
val () = println! ("foldleft = ", arrayptr_foldleft<int><int>(A, 3, 0, fopr))
}
//
val () =
{
var fopr = lam@(x: int, r: int): int => r * 10 + x
val () = println! ("foldright = ", arrayptr_foldright<int><int>(A, 3, fopr, 0))
}
//
val () = arrayptr_free(A)
//
')

")

#para("\

In practice, matrices (that is, two dimensional arrays) are also a
very common data structure. In ATS, there is a type constructor
#stacode("matrixptr"), which takes a type T and two integers m and n
to form the type #stacode("matrixptr(T, m, n)") for a matrix of m rows
and n columns storing elements of the type T. Like an array, a matrix is
linear and has to be freed by calling #dyncode("matrixptr_free"), and it
does not store its dimensions. For instance, the following function can be
called to create a matrix:

#sats2xhtml('\
//
fun{
a:t0p
} matrixptr_make_elt
  {m,n:int}
  (m: size_t m, n: size_t n, x: a):<!wrt> matrixptr(a, m, n)
// end of [matrixptr_make_elt]
//
')

Given two non-negative integers #dyncode("m") and #dyncode("n") and an
element #dyncode("x"), #dyncode("matrixptr_make_elt") returns a
matrix of dimension #dyncode("m") by #dyncode("n") in which
each of its cells is initialized with #dyncode("x").

")

#para("\

Please note that the so-called row-major representation is chosen for
the matrix contained in each created matrix. As the elements in
each row are stored adjacently in row-major representation, it can be
(much) more efficient to process the elements in the row-by-row fashion
(compared to the column-by-column fashion) due to the memory-cache effect.

")

#para("\

Like #dyncode("arrayptr_tabulate"), the function
#dyncode("matrixptr_tabulate") is often called to create a
matrix, where the template parameter #dyncode("matrix_tabulate$fopr") gives
the content of each cell:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
implement
matrix_tabulate$fopr<int>(i, j) = sz2i(i) * 10 + sz2i(j)
//
val N = matrixptr_tabulate<int>(i2sz(2), i2sz(3))
//
val () = (fprint_matrixptr<int>(stdout_ref, N, i2sz(2), i2sz(3)); print_newline())
val () = matrixptr_free(N)
//
')

")

#para("\

The functions #dyncode("matrixptr_get_at") and
#dyncode("matrixptr_set_at") are for fetching and updating the value
stored in a matrix at a given position (represented by a row
index and a column index; the number of columns is passed along, too, as the
matrix does not know it). The brackets #dyncode("[]") are overloaded with both
of them, as in the following code:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
val M = matrixptr_make_elt<int>(i2sz(2), i2sz(3), 0)
//
val () = M[0, 3, 1] := 5
val () = M[1, 3, 2] := 7
//
val () =
{
var fwork = lam@(x: int): void => print!(x, ";")
val () = matrixptr_foreach_clo<int>(M, 2, 3, fwork)
val () = println! ()
}
//
val () = matrixptr_free(M)
//
')

")

#para("\

Like #dyncode("arrayptr_foreach"), the following function
#dyncode("matrixptr_foreach_clo") applies to each element stored in a matrix a given
flat closure #dyncode("fwork") (the matrix is traversed row by row). The two loops are
written directly, since a closure that is passed by reference cannot be captured by
another one:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
extern
fun
{a:t0p}
matrixptr_foreach_clo
  {m,n:nat}
(
M: !matrixptr(a, m, n)
, nrow: int(m), ncol: int(n), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void
) : void // end of [matrixptr_foreach_clo]
//
implement
{a}(*tmp*)
matrixptr_foreach_clo
  {m,n}(M, nrow, ncol, fwork) =
loop1(M, 0, fwork) where
{
//
fun
loop2
{i:nat | i < m}{j:nat | j <= n}
(
M: !matrixptr(a, m, n), i: int(i), j: int(j), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void
) : void =
  if j < ncol then (fwork(M[i, ncol, j]); loop2(M, i, j+1, fwork)) else ()
//
fun
loop1
{i:nat | i <= m}
(
M: !matrixptr(a, m, n), i: int(i), fwork: &(a) -<clo1> void
) : void =
  if i < nrow then (loop2(M, i, 0, fwork); loop1(M, i+1, fwork)) else ()
//
} (* end of [matrixptr_foreach_clo] *)
//
')

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/08/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. It was compiled with #command("patscc")
(no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer, which reports no
leak. \

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture08"-->

